    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b53",
      "text": "Figure 1. Overview of RLHF, DPO, and TUR-DPO. RLHF trains a reward model from preference data and optimizes the policy via\n",
      "bbox": [
        54.89,
        183.93,
        541.44,
        193.14
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        219,
        735,
        2165,
        772
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 122,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b54",
      "text": "PPO with KL regularization. DPO replaces this pipeline with a direct, RL-free preference objective relative to a reference policy \u03c0ref.\nTUR-DPO augments DPO by incorporating lightweight reasoning topology, semantic, and uncertainty signals into a shaped reward and\nuncertainty-weighted DPO loss; the reference policy \u03c0ref may be updated via an exponential moving average (EMA). Right: Performance\nimprovement of TUR-DPO across representative benchmarks, measured against baseline using LLM judges and under human evaluation.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.16,
        193.91,
        543.01,
        232.95
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        775,
        2172,
        931
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 524,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b55",
      "text": "Bradley\u2013Terry (Huang et al., 2006) estimation and consis-\ntent preference recovery under standard assumptions and\nthat TUR-DPO optimizes a KL-regularized policy under a\nshaped reward, clarifying its relation to vanilla DPO.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        256.06,
        291.1,
        301.95
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        1024,
        1164,
        1207
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.05,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 223,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b56",
      "text": "In this work, we evaluate TUR-DPO across a range of rea-\nsoning, factual QA, summarization, and dialogue bench-\nmarks, including multimodal and long-context settings. We\nfocus on assessing whether incorporating structure and un-\ncertainty into preference optimization improves win-rates,\nfaithfulness, and calibration, while preserving the opera-\ntional simplicity of DPO and avoiding online rollouts. We\nadditionally include evaluations in multimodal and long-\ncontext settings. We empirically compare TUR-DPO with\nPPO-based RLHF on reasoning-centric and stylistic dia-\nlogue tasks, analyzing settings in which each approach is\nmost effective. The result preserves DPO simplicity while\nexplicitly rewarding structurally coherent and semantically\nsound solutions (1). TUR-DPO sits alongside recent RL-\nfree objectives including ORPO (Hong et al., 2024), KTO\n(Ethayarajh et al., 2024), and RRHF (Yuan et al., 2023), but\ndiffers in how it injects structured reasoning and calibrated\nuncertainty directly into a DPO-style objective.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.11,
        309.88,
        291.1,
        523.12
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        1239,
        1164,
        2092
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.02,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 1029,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b57",
      "text": "The most important contributions of this paper are:\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        531.09,
        258.32,
        541.06
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2124,
        1033,
        2164
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 51,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b58",
      "text": "\u2022 We introduce TUR-DPO, a structure- and uncertainty-\naware framework for preference-based alignment that\nincorporates\nlightweight\nreasoning\ntopologies\nand\nuncertainty-weighted preference losses to reduce sensitiv-\nity to noisy or brittle comparisons.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        550.94,
        291.1,
        608.8
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2203,
        1164,
        2435
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 251,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b59",
      "text": "\u2022 We propose an RL-free logit-level augmentation of DPO\nthat integrates a learned, factorized reward over struc-\ntural and semantic signals, and we provide theoretical\nconnections to instance-weighted Bradley-Terry models\nand KL-regularized policy optimization.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        617.31,
        291.1,
        675.09
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2469,
        1164,
        2700
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 261,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b60",
      "text": "\u2022 We conduct an empirical study of reasoning, QA, sum-\nmarization, and dialogue that is validated with both au-\ntomated and human evaluations and, furthermore, is ex-\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        683.52,
        291.1,
        717.49
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2734,
        1164,
        2869
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 166,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b61",
      "text": "tended to multimodal and long-context tasks.\n",
      "bbox": [
        315.91,
        256.13,
        495.63,
        266.09
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1263,
        1024,
        1982,
        1064
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 44,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b62",
      "text": "\u2022 We further compare TUR-DPO against recent RL-free\npreference optimization methods (ORPO (Hong et al.,\n2024), SimPO (Meng et al., 2024), KTO (Ethayarajh et al.,\n2024), and IPO (Azar et al., 2023)), and show consistent\nimprovements across head-to-head evaluations.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        274.93,
        542.68,
        332.79
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1099,
        2170,
        1331
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 264,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b63",
      "text": "Unlike prior reward-model-based or PPO-style approaches,\nTUR-DPO operates entirely within a preference optimiza-\ntion framework without online rollouts or policy-gradient\nupdates. Rather than serving as a universal replacement for\npreference-based alignment methods, TUR-DPO is intended\nas a complementary approach that is most effective when\npreferences reflect multi-step reasoning or factual ground-\ning, and when supervision is noisy or brittle. A more de-\ntailed discussion of related work is provided in Appendix A.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.13,
        342.77,
        543.19,
        448.33
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1228,
        1371,
        2172,
        1793
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.94,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 521,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b64",
      "text": "2. Methodology and Theoretical Foundations\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        465.34,
        536.42,
        477.3
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1861,
        2145,
        1909
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 42,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b65",
      "text": "TUR-DPO is designed to preserve the simplicity and sta-\nbility of direct preference optimization while injecting two\nmissing signals: explicit reasoning structure and calibrated\nuncertainty. The training loop mirrors DPO. We maintain a\nsupervised policy \u03c0\u03b8 and a reference \u03c0ref that is either fixed\nor updated slowly by EMA. The data consist of pairwise\npreferences D = {(xi, y+\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.13,
        486.26,
        543.09,
        568.64
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1228,
        1945,
        2172,
        2274
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 378,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p2_b66",
      "text": "i , y\u2212\n",
      "bbox": [
        407.3,
        555.82,
        430.16,
        570.57
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1629,
